== Introduction

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(style: "italic")[But when you pray, go into your closet, shut your door, and pray to your Father, who is in secret. And your Father, who sees in secret, will reward you openly.]

    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[— Matt 6:6]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

These words of our Savior are plain and are to be taken literally, not allegorically, for He speaks of shutting the door of the room. In this chapter there is a clear contrast between the Pharisees praying in the synagogues and on the street corners, and others praying in secret.

In the text, you have a positive precept for every Christian to pray alone: “But when #emph[you] pray.” He does not say, “when #emph[you#super[+]] pray,” in the plural, but “when #emph[you] pray,” in the singular, “go into your closet,” as speaking not so much of a joint duty of many praying together, as of a duty which each person is to do alone. The command in the text sends us to the closet as well as to the church. And he is a hypocrite through and through who chooses the one and neglects the other, for by this he tells the world that he cares for neither and makes neither a matter of conscience. He who puts on the garb of religion in public to gain himself a great name among men, and at the same time lives like an atheist at home, will at last be stripped by God and presented before all the world as a most egregious hypocrite.

Bellarmine and some others turn the text into an allegory. They say that there are two allegories in these words. First, the door of the room is the senses: “shut your door,” that is, they say, your senses, so that vain imaginations and worldly thoughts do not distract your mind in praying. Secondly, the door, they say, is our mouth: “shut your door,” that is, your lips, they say, and let your prayer be like the prayer of Hannah, conceived in your mind, but not uttered with your mouth. It is usual with papists, and other monkish men who lie in wait to deceive, to turn the blessed Scriptures into a nose of wax under pretense of allegories and mysteries. Origen was a great admirer of allegories. By the strength of his talents and his fanciful mind, he turned most of the Scriptures into allegories. And by the just judgment of God on him, he foolishly understood and absurdly applied Matt 19:12 literally, “others have made themselves eunuchs for the sake of the kingdom of heaven,” and so castrated himself. And indeed he might as well have plucked out one of his eyes on the same account, because Christ says, “It is better for you to enter life with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the fire of hell” (Matt 18:9). In all ages heretics have commonly defended their heresies by turning Scriptures into allegories. The apostle speaks of men who, denying the resurrection of the body, turn all the testimonies of the resurrection into an allegory, meaning by them only the spiritual resurrection of the soul from sin. Of this sort were Hymenaeus and Philetus, who destroyed the faith of some, saying “that the resurrection has already occurred” (2 Tim 2:17–18). And are there not many among us who turn the whole history of the Bible into an allegory, and who turn Christ, sin, death, the soul, hell, heaven, and all into an allegory? Many have perverted and many do pervert the Scriptures miserably by turning them into vain and groundless allegories. Some fanciful minds have expounded paradise to be the soul, the man to be the mind, the woman to be the sense, the serpent to be delight, the tree of the knowledge of good and evil to be wisdom, and the rest of the trees to be the virtues and endowments of the mind. O friends, it is dangerous to bring in allegories where the Scripture does not clearly and plainly warrant them, and to take figuratively words which should be taken in their proper sense.

The word ταμεῖον, which in the text is rendered #emph[closet], has only three common meanings among Greek authors. First, it may be taken for a secret room, or a private and locked parlor; secondly, for a pantry or cupboard to keep food in; thirdly, for a locked chest or cupboard in which treasure is usually kept.

The best and most judicious interpreters I have cast my eye on, both of earlier and later date, all expound my text of private prayer in secluded places, and I agree with them. And so the main doctrine that I will gather from the words is this:

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[Doctrine: Closet prayer or private prayer is an indispensable duty that Christ Himself has laid on all who are not willing to lie under the woeful brand of being hypocrites.]

I urge you#super[+] seriously to lay these five things to heart:

#emph[First], if any prayer is a duty, then secret prayer must be a duty, for secret prayer is as much prayer as any other prayer is prayer. And secret prayer prepares and fits the soul for family prayer and for public prayer. Secret prayer sweetly inclines and strongly disposes a Christian to all other religious duties and services.

Therefore, #emph[secondly], if secret prayer is not an indispensable duty that lies on you, by what authority does conscience so upbraid you, and so accuse you, and so condemn you, and so terrify you, as it often does for the neglect of this duty?

#emph[Thirdly], was it ever the way or method of God to promise again and again a reward, an open reward, for a work or service which He Himself never commanded? Surely not. Now, to this duty of secret prayer the Lord has again and again promised an open reward (Matt 6:6, 18). And therefore, without any doubt, this is a duty binding on all Christians.

#emph[Fourthly], our Savior in the text takes it for granted that every child of God will be frequent in praying to His heavenly Father; and therefore He encourages them all the more in the work of secret prayer. “When you#super[+] pray”—as if He had said, “I know you can as well hear without ears, and live without food, and fight without hands, and walk without feet, as you are able to live without prayer. And therefore, when you#super[+] go to wait on God, or to pay your heavenly Father a visit, go into your closet, and shut your doors,” etc.

#emph[Fifthly], if closet prayer is not an indispensable duty that Christ has laid on all His people, why does Satan oppose it so much? Why does he labor so industriously and so tirelessly to discourage Christians in it, and to draw Christians away from it? Certainly Satan would never make such a fierce and constant war as he does on private prayer if it were not a necessary duty, a real duty, and a soul-enriching duty. But you will find more of this in the following discourse, and therefore let this touch suffice for the present.

Now, these five things very clearly and plainly demonstrate that to hold fellowship with God secretly and alone is the undoubted duty of every Christian. But for a fuller opening and confirmation of this great and important point, I will lay down these twenty arguments or considerations.
